<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Oil &amp; gas&nbsp; ● &nbsp;Leak detection &amp; repair</p>
      <h2>One leak, three kinds of exposure.</h2>
      <p>A methane leak is a process safety hazard, lost product, and compliance finding all at once. That's why your LDAR program can't live in a spreadsheet — the same finding has to answer to your staff, operations, and emissions numbers.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div><img class="line-icon" src="/static/img/icons/process-safety.svg" width="40" height="40" alt=""><h3 class="h4">Process Safety</h3><p>Escaping gas is a flammability and exposure hazard for the crews working around it. Fast, verified repairs — not just logged findings — are what actually protect people in the field.</p></div>
      <div><img class="line-icon" src="/static/img/icons/lost-product.svg" width="40" height="40" alt=""><h3 class="h4">Lost Product</h3><p>Every day a leak stays open, methane you could sell goes to the atmosphere. Closing leaks faster is operational discipline that pays for itself.</p></div>
      <div><img class="line-icon" src="/static/img/icons/regulatory-emissions.svg" width="40" height="40" alt=""><h3 class="h4">Regulatory &amp; Emissions</h3><p>Repair clocks start the moment a leak is found. Miss a first-attempt deadline or Delay of Repair justification, and a routine finding becomes an external issue. And open days add directly to your emission inventory.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

